.TH MU2E-VAULT-IPMI 1 "2026-09-17" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
mu2e-vault-ipmi \- verify the Vault secret holding the Mu2e DAQ BMC credentials
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mu2e-vault-ipmi
.RB [ \-\-path
.IR REL ]
.RB [ \-\-addr
.IR URL ]
.RB [ \-\-fields ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-fallback ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Checks that the recovery tools will be able to read the BMC credentials, which
live at
.I td/scd/experiments/mu2e/ipmi/config
on
.IR https://ssivault.fnal.gov:8200 .
.PP
The purpose is to find a credential problem
.I before
an outage rather than during one. The exact field names in the live secret
could not be confirmed from the repositories, so
.I vault.ipmi_user_field
and
.I vault.ipmi_password_field
are configurable, the client accepts the obvious synonyms, and this tool
reports which field was actually used.
.PP
No secret value is ever printed. The output gives the field names present, the
field selected, the username, the password's length, and where the credential
came from \(em enough to confirm the configuration and nothing that needs
protecting.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-path " REL"
Secret path relative to
.IR vault.base_path .
Default:
.IR vault.ipmi_path ", normally " ipmi/config .
Note that
.I ipmi
is a folder in the KV tree, not the secret; reading the folder returns
nothing, which looks identical to an empty secret. When a path holds no
secret this tool lists what is under it, so the two cases are
distinguishable.
.TP
.BI \-\-addr " URL"
Override
.IR vault.addr .
.TP
.B \-\-fields
Print only the field names present in the secret.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-fallback
Fail rather than falling back to the local password file. Use this to test
Vault itself, rather than testing whether a recovery could proceed.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the report as JSON.
.SH FALLBACK
When Vault is unreachable and
.I vault.allow_file_fallback
is set, the tools fall back to
.I ~/.ipmipasswd
\(em the file the existing
.I mu2edaq-operations
scripts already read. This matters more here than it would elsewhere: a
site-wide power event is exactly when the Vault server may also be down, and
being unable to reach Vault must not be the reason the DAQ cannot be powered
back on. The fallback is recorded in the run, and this tool reports it in the
.I Source
line.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
A Vault token is needed. Obtain one with:
.RS
.EX
vault login \-method=ldap \-address=https://ssivault.fnal.gov:8200
.EE
.RE
.PP
which caches the token at
.IR ~/.vault-token .
The tools run that command themselves when no usable token is found, unless
.I vault.auto_login
is false.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Usable BMC credentials were obtained.
.TP
.B 1
They were not; the reason is printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.EX
mu2e-vault-ipmi
mu2e-vault-ipmi \-\-fields
mu2e-vault-ipmi \-\-no\-fallback \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-ipmi-tool (1),
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery.yaml (5),
.BR vault (1)
.SH AUTHOR
A. Norman <anorman@fnal.gov>, Mu2e DAQ Operations.
